CIPHER LABS - PHASED BUILD PROMPT (v3)
======================================

This version replaces Cipher_Labs_Phased_Build_Prompt_v2.txt and Cipher_Labs_Full_Build_Prompt.txt. Do not use the older files.

Changes from v2 (all corrections are built into the text below):
• Tauri project lives inside frontend/ (frontend/src-tauri). One package root, one set of build commands.
• Sidecar is launched from Rust with the shell plugin, and the capability file grants shell:allow-spawn for that sidecar only.
• Signup creates the profile row through a database trigger on auth.users, so it does not depend on a session or client-side insert.
• Supabase is the permanent source of truth. A small offline queue holds pending-sync metadata only (not a database), with idempotent sync.
• Simulation results use simulation-specific labels. They are never shown as physical erasure verification.
• Device records store only OS name, OS version, app version and the app-generated device ID. No CPU or hardware identifiers.
• Phase 0 checks Supabase configuration without printing any secret value.


PART 0 - HOW TO USE THIS PROMPT
-------------------------------

1. Paste PART 1 to PART 9 (locked decisions, design, auth, database, backend, packaging, offline rules, release rules) at the start of every session. This is the shared specification.
2. Paste ONLY the phase block you are building from PART 11 (Phase 0, 1, 2 or 3).
3. The builder writes complete files for that phase, runs the phase tests, and reports using PART 12.
4. Do not start the next phase until the current gate is passed and reviewed.
5. Fix errors inside the phase in which they are found. Use the actual error log as input. Do not rewrite unrelated code.
6. A phase is not complete until its gate passes. A passing build is not the same as a working .exe.


PART 1 - LOCKED DECISIONS (DO NOT CHANGE)
-----------------------------------------

Platform
• Target: Windows 10 (21H2 or later) and Windows 11, 64-bit only.
• Linux and macOS are out of scope. Do not add platform branches for them.

Stack
• Desktop shell: Tauri v2 (Rust stable, tauri and tauri-plugin-shell v2).
• Frontend: React 18, TypeScript (strict), Vite, Tailwind CSS, Motion for React, lucide-react, react-router-dom, @supabase/supabase-js.
• Local backend: Python 3.11, FastAPI, Pydantic v2, uvicorn, packaged with PyInstaller as a Tauri sidecar.
• Database, authentication and authorisation: Supabase only (PostgreSQL, Supabase Auth, Row Level Security). This is the permanent source of truth for every user record.
• Machine learning (Phase 2 only): scikit-learn, real trained-model outputs only.

Project layout (exact; see PART 7)
• Everything for the desktop app lives under frontend/. The Tauri project is frontend/src-tauri/.
• The repository root holds only backend/, supabase/, scripts/, docs/, README.md, .gitignore, .env.example.

Local storage rule
• No SQLite and no other local database for user records.
• The only local persistence is: (a) the app-data folder, (b) the offline pending-sync queue (PART 8), a small JSON file holding metadata only, removed entry by entry after successful sync.

Keys and secrets
• The app may hold the Supabase URL and the anon (publishable) key only.
• The service-role key must never appear in the frontend, the sidecar, the installer, logs, or the repository. It is used only in one-off admin scripts that are never packaged.
• The backend forwards the user's access token to Supabase so Row Level Security applies. The backend verifies the token before serving any protected endpoint.

Scope of this release (Phases 0 to 3)
• No real modification of any file or folder outside the app-data folder.
• No real erasure of user data.
• No scanning of internal or attached drives.
• No raw device or disk access of any kind, not even behind a flag.
• Erasure is SIMULATION ONLY (Phase 1). It never performs a physical erasure and never writes to any file.
• Recovery works ONLY on test disk images the app creates inside its app-data folder (Phase 2).
• Real destructive erasure and real drive recovery are a later release with its own design review. No code path for them may be added here.

Privacy
• Desktop app: no website cookies and no cookie banner. A cookie banner belongs to the later public website.
• Privacy preferences are stored in the user's profile in Supabase and are read by the backend (log retention and export). They are real settings, not decoration.


PART 2 - PRODUCT IDENTITY AND CORE RULES
----------------------------------------

Name: Cipher Labs
Tagline: SECURE. RECOVER. VERIFY.
Descriptor: Digital Forensics and Data Sanitization

Rules the UI and backend must always respect
• Execution and verification are separate states. "Completed" is never shown as "Verified" unless verification passed.
• Simulation results are never labelled "Verified" or "Erased". They use the simulation labels in PART 2.1.
• A recovery candidate is "Detected" or "Validated" until its output is written and re-validated. Only then is it "Recovered".
• A hash match is an integrity comparison. It never proves secure erasure.
• Flash media (SSD, USB, memory card) cannot be fully verified by software. Any future erasure report on such media must say so.
• A failed or unverifiable outcome is shown as failed or UNVERIFIED. Success is shown only with a backend result that confirms it.
• A database outage never turns a verified result into a failure, and never shows zeros that look like real data. Show "Unavailable" with a Retry action.

PART 2.1 - SIMULATION LABELS (EXACT WORDING)
• Operation type label: "Simulation".
• Verification status values for simulations: SIMULATION_CHECK_PASSED or SIMULATION_CHECK_FAILED.
• UI text for SIMULATION_CHECK_PASSED: "Simulation check passed. No file changes were detected. This is not physical erasure verification."
• UI text for SIMULATION_CHECK_FAILED: "Simulation check failed. A file change was detected. Review the details before doing anything else."
• Report text: "This is a simulation. No physical erasure was performed. The check confirms only that the simulated targets were not changed."
• Forbidden words for simulation results anywhere in UI or reports: "Verified", "Erased", "Sanitized", "Destroyed".


