PART 4 - AUTHENTICATION AND SIGNUP (SUPABASE)
---------------------------------------------

Signup fields (in order)
1. First name (required, 1 to 60 characters)
2. Last name (required, 1 to 60 characters)
3. Email (required, valid format)
4. Phone number (optional; if entered: starts with +, then 8 to 15 digits, no spaces). Helper text: "Optional. Used for your profile only." Not used for sign-in, no SMS is sent.
5. Password (minimum 8 characters, at least one letter and one digit, strength indicator)
6. Confirm password (must match)
7. "Create account" button
8. "Continue with Google" button. Enabled only when Supabase Google OAuth is configured. Otherwise disabled with: "Google sign-in is available after Supabase Google login is configured."

Signup sends first name, last name and phone as user metadata (options.data) in the Supabase signUp call. The database trigger in PART 5 creates the profiles row from this metadata. The app does NOT insert the profile itself during signup, so signup works whether or not a session is returned.

After signup
• If email confirmation is enabled, Supabase returns no session. Show "Check your email to verify your account" with a Resend button (60-second cooldown).
• If a session is returned, go to the dashboard.
• After the first successful sign-in, the app calls GET /api/profile. If the profile row is missing (for example, the trigger failed on an older account), the app calls POST /api/profile/ensure, which creates the row from the user's metadata with the user's own token, and shows the result. If that fails, show the error with a Retry button. Never leave a user on a broken dashboard without explanation.

Login: email, password, "Sign in", Google button (same rule), "Forgot password?", link to Sign up. Failure message: "Email or password is incorrect." Never say which field was wrong.

Forgot password: email field, "Send reset link". Always show "If an account exists for this email, a reset link has been sent." Reset page uses the Supabase recovery session to set a new password.

Sessions
• The Supabase JavaScript client holds the session. Do not write custom token storage.
• Expired session: redirect to /login with "Your session has expired. Sign in again."
• Sign out calls Supabase signOut and clears all client state.
• Backend protected endpoints require "Authorization: Bearer <access token>". The backend verifies the token with Supabase and returns UNAUTHENTICATED for missing or invalid tokens.
• Never log passwords, tokens, or the Authorization header.

Supabase Auth settings the README must list
• Email provider enabled. Email confirmation: on or off, documented, both must work.
• Site URL and redirect URLs for password reset (tauri localhost URLs for development and the production asset origin used by the app).
• Google provider: optional; documented steps.


PART 5 - DATABASE (SUPABASE POSTGRESQL)
---------------------------------------

Migrations live in supabase/migrations/ as numbered SQL files and must apply in order to an empty project. The README gives the apply steps (SQL editor or Supabase CLI).

5.1 Profile creation trigger (0001_profiles_trigger.sql)

Create the profile from auth metadata so signup never depends on a session:

create table public.profiles (
id uuid primary key references auth.users(id) on delete cascade,
first_name text not null check (char_length(first_name) between 1 and 60),
last_name text not null check (char_length(last_name) between 1 and 60),
phone text null check (phone is null or phone ~ '^\+[0-9]{8,15}$'),
email text not null,
preferences jsonb not null default '{}'::jsonb,
created_at timestamptz not null default now(),
updated_at timestamptz not null default now()
);

create or replace function public.handle_new_user()
returns trigger
language plpgsql
security definer
set search_path = ''
as $$
begin
insert into public.profiles (id, first_name, last_name, phone, email)
values (
new.id,
coalesce(new.raw_user_meta_data->>'first_name', ''),
coalesce(new.raw_user_meta_data->>'last_name', ''),
nullif(new.raw_user_meta_data->>'phone', ''),
new.email
)
on conflict (id) do nothing;
return new;
end;
$$;

revoke execute on function public.handle_new_user() from public, anon, authenticated;

create trigger on_auth_user_created
after insert on auth.users
for each row execute function public.handle_new_user();

Notes the builder must apply:
• The function is security definer, so it can insert while RLS is active. Do NOT use FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY on profiles.
• If first name or last name is missing or invalid, the check constraint rejects the insert and signup fails. This is intended: the account is not left without a profile. The frontend validates the same rules first, so this should not happen in normal use.
• The phone check uses the same format as the frontend and the backend.

5.2 Grants and RLS (0002_rls.sql)
• Enable row level security on every table in this release.
• Grants: revoke all on all tables in schema public from anon. Grant select, insert, update, delete to authenticated only where the policies below allow it. Do not grant anything to anon.
• profiles: select and update where id = auth.uid(). Insert allowed for authenticated where id = auth.uid(), used only by the repair path POST /api/profile/ensure. No delete policy (profile deletion comes with account deletion in a later release).
• devices, operations, operation_events, reports, audit_log: select, insert, update, delete where user_id = auth.uid(). Except audit_log: select and insert only, no update and no delete policy.
• Every insert policy also checks user_id = auth.uid() in the with check clause.
• Write supabase/tests/rls_test.sql that creates two test users, proves each can read only their own rows, proves user B cannot update or delete user A's rows, and proves audit_log rows cannot be updated or deleted.

