7.6 frontend/src-tauri/src/main.rs (sidecar launch, the required shape)

The sidecar is started from Rust with the shell plugin's spawn API. The shell plugin is initialised explicitly. The capability in 7.7 grants shell:allow-spawn for this sidecar only.

use std::sync::Mutex;
use std::time::Duration;
use tauri::{Manager, RunEvent};
use tauri_plugin_shell::{process::{CommandChild, CommandEvent}, ShellExt};

struct Backend(Mutex<Option<CommandChild>>);

// Compile-time values, read from the environment at build time by scripts/build_backend.ps1.
// If unset, the backend starts in CONFIG_MISSING mode.
const SUPABASE_URL: Option<&str> = option_env!("CIPHER_SUPABASE_URL");
const SUPABASE_ANON_KEY: Option<&str> = option_env!("CIPHER_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY");

fn main() {
tauri::Builder::default()
.plugin(tauri_plugin_shell::init())
.manage(Backend(Mutex::new(None)))
.setup(|app| {
let data_dir = app.path().app_data_dir()?;
std::fs::create_dir_all(&data_dir)?;
let mut cmd = app
.shell()
.sidecar("cipher-backend")?
.env("CIPHER_APP_DATA", data_dir.to_string_lossy().to_string());
if let (Some(url), Some(key)) = (SUPABASE_URL, SUPABASE_ANON_KEY) {
cmd = cmd.env("SUPABASE_URL", url).env("SUPABASE_ANON_KEY", key);
}
let (mut rx, child) = cmd.spawn()?;
*app.state::<Backend>().0.lock().unwrap() = Some(child);

// Forward sidecar output to the Tauri log. Do not print secrets.
tauri::async_runtime::spawn(async move {
while let Some(event) = rx.recv().await {
if let CommandEvent::Stdout(line) | CommandEvent::Stderr(line) = event {
log::info!("backend: {}", String::from_utf8_lossy(&line));
}
}
});

// Wait for /api/health (up to 15 s). If it fails, show an error and exit.
// Implement with a blocking HTTP GET to http://127.0.0.1:8765/api/health every 250 ms.
// On failure, show a dialog "The Cipher Labs service could not start. Check the log at {log path}." and exit.
Ok(())
})
.build(tauri::generate_context!())
.expect("error while building Cipher Labs")
.run(|app, event| {
if let RunEvent::Exit = event {
if let Some(child) = app.state::<Backend>().0.lock().unwrap().take() {
let _ = child.kill();
}
}
});
}

Notes for the builder
• The health wait needs an HTTP client. Add the minimal crate the builder chooses (for example ureq) and list it in Cargo.toml. Do not add a shell permission for it; the request is made by Rust, not the webview.
• Logging crate: use the log crate with tauri's logging if the scaffold provides it, or drop the log lines. Do not print secret values anywhere.
• Environment variables are compile-time values from build_backend.ps1. The anon key is a public value, but it is still never printed or logged.
• The builder confirms each API name (sidecar, spawn, env, kill, CommandEvent variants) against the Tauri v2 docs for the installed version, and reports any change.

