PART 8 - OFFLINE PENDING-SYNC QUEUE (PHASE 3)
---------------------------------------------

Decision
• Supabase is the permanent source of truth.
• The offline queue is a temporary holding list of records that could not be written yet. It is not a database and holds no user files.
• The queue is a JSON file in app data, written atomically (write to a temporary file, then rename).

What the queue may contain
• Operation metadata and report metadata only: operation_type, mode, target_reference, method, statuses, timestamps, idempotency_key, report_sha256 and the report JSON.
• It never contains file contents, tokens, passwords, or the Supabase keys.

Entry format
{ "queue_id": "uuid", "record_type": "operation|report", "idempotency_key": "...",
"payload": { ... }, "created_at": "...", "attempts": 0, "last_error_code": null,
"mac": "hex HMAC-SHA256 of the entry" }
The HMAC key is random, created on first run, and stored in app data as queue.key. The HMAC detects accidental corruption and casual editing of the queue file. It does not stop a determined person with full access to the user's account, and the README states this limit.

Sync rules
• Sync runs at startup and every 60 seconds while signed in and online, with bounded exponential backoff after failures (1, 2, 4, up to 5 minutes).
• Each entry is sent with its idempotency_key. The server enforces unique (user_id, idempotency_key), so a repeated send does not create a duplicate.
• An entry is removed from the queue only after a success response, or after a conflict response that confirms the same record already exists.
• A failed entry stays in the queue, with attempts incremented and last_error_code set.
• An entry whose MAC does not match is moved to queue.rejected.json and shown to the user as "Pending record could not be verified and was not synced." It is never sent.
• The status shown is "Synchronization pending" when entries exist, and "Synchronization failed" after repeated failures with the last error code.

Tests (Phase 3)
• Restart during a pending sync: the entry is sent once after restart and not duplicated.
• Network failure during sync: the entry stays queued and is sent later.
• Successful sync removes the entry from the queue.
• A tampered entry is rejected and never sent.
• Sending the same entry twice leaves one record in Supabase.
• A write to the queue file that is interrupted leaves the previous valid queue intact.


